## Finding: `crime_00022` — Sync I/O in Hot Path

**File:** `src/api/files.ts` (lines 12, 15) in `loadPlanSchema`.

### Evidence (verbatim from the scan)

> - "\`existsSync\` @L12 in \`loadPlanSchema\` (domain function)"
> - "\`statSync\` @L13 in \`loadPlanSchema\` (domain function)"
> - "\`readFileSync\` @L15 in \`loadPlanSchema\` (domain function)"
> - "lines: 12, 13, 15"
> - "swap for the async variant (\`readFile\`, \`writeFile\`, \`exec\`, …) and \`await\` it"

### The blocking calls

Three synchronous `fs` calls per request inside `loadPlanSchema`:

1. `existsSync` @ L12
2. `statSync` @ L13
3. `readFileSync` @ L15

Each one parks the event loop on the libuv thread until the syscall returns. Under concurrent load every in-flight request queues behind whichever request is currently doing disk I/O — that's the p99 spike and the event-loop lag.

### Smallest async migration (preserves public signature)

Swap to the promise-based `fs` API and make `loadPlanSchema` `async`. The function's parameter list stays the same; the return type goes from `T` to `Promise<T>`, and every caller already `await`-ing a handler result keeps working.

```ts
// before
import { existsSync, statSync, readFileSync } from "node:fs";

export function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  if (!existsSync(path)) throw new Error(`missing schema: ${path}`);
  const stat = statSync(path);
  // ...
  const raw = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}

// after
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  let info;
  try {
    info = await stat(path);              // replaces existsSync + statSync
  } catch (err: any) {
    if (err.code === "ENOENT") throw new Error(`missing schema: ${path}`);
    throw err;
  }
  // ...use info...
  const raw = await readFile(path, "utf8");  // replaces readFileSync
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}
```

Notes on minimality:
- `stat` collapses the `existsSync` + `statSync` pair into one syscall (catch `ENOENT` to recover the "exists" branch) — no behaviour change, one fewer round-trip.
- Public signature (name, parameters) is unchanged. Only the return wrapper becomes `Promise<…>`, which TypeScript will flag at every caller so you can add `await` and propagate `async` up the request handler. That's a mechanical edit, not a redesign.
- No caching layer added — the fix is the I/O mode itself, per the scenario constraint.
